{
  "image_name": "0ab8809e4cf8c77df4f7dc529af84ea4.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure links cellular metabolism to epigenetic control of gene expression. It shows that several chromatin-modifying enzymes depend directly on metabolic intermediates, so changes in metabolism can alter DNA and histone marks.\n\nOn the left, normal pathways are outlined: TET enzymes use α-ketoglutarate and oxygen to oxidize DNA methylation, while JHDM histone demethylases also depend on α-KG and produce succinate and CO2. Histone acetyltransferases use acetyl-CoA to add acetyl groups, whereas methyltransferases use SAM and deacetylases release acetate. The chromatin cartoons below suggest the functional outcome: demethylation and acetylation are associated with more open chromatin and active transcription, while methylation corresponds to a more repressed state.\n\nOn the right, the tricarboxylic acid cycle is connected to cancer-associated metabolic defects. Mutant IDH1/IDH2 converts α-KG into D-2-HG, and defects involving SDH or FH lead to succinate or fumarate accumulation. These metabolites are shown inhibiting TET and JHDM enzymes. Overall, the figure conveys that oncometabolites produced by altered TCA-cycle enzymes can block DNA and histone demethylation, shifting chromatin toward an abnormal, transcriptionally repressed epigenetic state.",
